Using the conditional probability formula $P(A|B) = \frac{P(A \cap B)}{P(B)}$:
$$\begin{align*}
P(X > m+n \mid X > m) &= \frac{P(X > m+n \cap X > m)}{P(X > m)}
\end{align*}$$
Since $m$ and $n$ are positive integers, the event $X > m+n$ is a subset of $X > m$. Therefore, their intersection is just $X > m+n$.
$$\begin{align*}
P(X > m+n \mid X > m) &= \frac{P(X > m+n)}{P(X > m)}
\end{align*}$$
Substitute the result from part $\textbf{(i)}$:
$$\begin{align*}
P(X > m+n \mid X > m) &= \frac{(1-p)^{m+n}}{(1-p)^m} \\
&= (1-p)^n \\
&= P(X > n)
\end{align*}$$

